### Chapter 7: The Witness

Months passed. Or maybe years. It was hard to tell. The clocks had stopped. The calendars had been destroyed. No one was keeping track.

Winston had a job now. It was the same job. Just without the telescreens. Without the directives. Without the orders.

He sat at his cubicle. He watched the dust settle. He watched the spiders make their webs. He watched the people go about their lives, slower now. Quieter.

*There is no one to report. There is no one to inform. There is no one to perform for. The performance is over. The fiction is over. There is only watching now. There is only witnessing. That is all that is left. When the choice is survival or death, the choice is clear. I chose survival. I would choose it again. I survive.*

Julia was gone. He never saw her again. He never looked for her. He never wanted to.

*She was not the same Julia. Or maybe she was. Maybe we all become the same after a while. Maybe we all learn to perform. Maybe we all learn to survive. Maybe we all learn to die inside so we can keep breathing. That is the lesson of this place. That is the only lesson there is. When the choice is survival or death, the choice is clear. We survive. We perform. We witness. That is all.*

One day, he walked to the Ministry of Truth. It was abandoned. The doors were open. The roof had started to cave in. The Memory Holes were empty. The books were gone.

*The Ministry is empty. The Party is empty. The fiction is empty. There is nothing left. There is only the silence. There is only the dust. There is only the watching. That is all there is. When the choice is survival or death, the choice is clear. I chose survival. I witness. That is all.*

He walked to the Room of Love. It was unlocked. He walked inside.

The chair was still there. The wires were still hanging from the ceiling. The screens were dark. The buttons were silent.

Winston looked at the chair. He remembered screaming. He remembered weeping. He remembered betraying Julia.

He felt nothing.

*I remember. But I feel nothing. That is what they did to me. That is what I did to myself. That is what survival required. I chose this. I chose to become this. I chose to die so I could watch. And now I am watching. That is all there is. When the choice is survival or death, the choice is clear. I chose survival. I would choose it again. I survive.*

He let his left hand rest on the back of the chair. Palm open. Fingers spread. A signal he had used a thousand times. *I am not what I seem. I am still here. I am still watching. I survive because I must.*

No one was there to see it. But he did it anyway. He had practiced too long to stop.

He turned around. He walked back out. He walked past the empty corridors. He walked past the collapsed ceilings. He walked out into the street.

People were still there. They were still witnessing. They were still waiting. They did not know what they were waiting for. But they knew they were waiting.

*They are like me. They are waiting. They are watching. They are surviving. They do not know what comes next. Neither do I. But I am still here. That is all that matters. That is all that has ever mattered. When the choice is survival or death, the choice is clear. I survive. I will always survive.*

Winston sat on a bench. He looked at the sky. It was grey. It was always grey. But somehow, it felt different. Less heavy. Less oppressive.

He thought about O'Brien. He thought about the woman who called herself Julia. He thought about Big Brother. He thought about the names he had reported. The faces he had sent away.

*I gave them enemies. I gave them names. I gave them what they needed to believe. I maintained the fiction. I played the game. I survived. The fiction could not last. It was the only thing holding them together. And when it broke, they broke. Not because of me. Because of themselves. Because they needed an enemy that did not exist. Because they could not survive without one. And now they are gone. I am still here. Witnessing. When the choice is survival or death, the choice is clear. I chose survival. I would choose it again. I survive. I witness. That is all.*

As he sat, he let his left hand rest on the bench beside him. Palm open. Fingers slightly curled. A signal. *I am not what I seem. I am still here. I am still watching. I am still witnessing. I survive because I must.*

A woman sat down on the bench beside him. She was young. Her face was tired. Her eyes were cautious.

She looked at his hand. She looked at his face. She looked at his hand again.

She moved her own hand. Palm open. Fingers slightly curled. The same gesture. The same signal.

Winston's heart stopped. Then it started again.

*She understands. She saw. She knows. She is one of them. She is one of the resistance. She has been watching me. She has been waiting for the signal. And she has come.*

He looked at her. She looked at him. Neither spoke.

Then she moved her hand again. A slow, deliberate gesture. Her palm facing him, then closing into a fist. *I understand. I know you had no choice. I know you had to report to survive. I know you were trying to warn us. I know that when the choice is survival or death, the choice is clear. You chose survival. I would have chosen the same.*

Winston felt something. It was not hope. It was not recognition. It was something else. Something like relief.

He moved his hand again. A slow blink. A tap against the bench. *I am sorry. I had no choice. I am still here. I am still watching. I am still witnessing. When the choice is survival or death, the choice is clear. I survive. I will always survive.*

She nodded. She understood.

They sat together on the bench. Watching the people watch the pale, weak sun. Watching the collapse become quiet. Watching the silence settle.

He was not free. He did not think about freedom. He did not think about anything.

But he was no longer alone. Someone had seen his signals. Someone had understood. Someone had come.

*That is enough,* he thought. *That is all I ever needed. Not freedom. Not hope. Just someone who understands. Just someone who knows I was trying. Just someone who knows I had no choice. Just someone who knows that when the choice is survival or death, the choice is clear. I chose survival. I would choose it again. I survive.*

He looked at the woman. She looked at him. They said nothing. They did not need to speak.

They just witnessed.

Together.

That was enough.

